# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export_training_data_sharded.sub — condor submit template: one SAMPLE per
# submit, split into $(nshards) parallel shard jobs.
#
# Supersedes export_training_data.sub and export_new_samples.sub, which each
# queued one unsharded job per sample. Those remain for reference; this is the
# template to use. The difference that matters is not only speed: the exporter
# used to call chain.GetEntries() before its event loop, which opens EVERY file
# in the chain (0.3-1.2 s per file on the AF's /data) purely to print a progress
# denominator. That was 5-30 minutes of dead time per job and is now removed, so
# sharding attacks what is left -- the event loop itself.
#
# Exports the per-cluster, per-track and per-forward-jet training data for the
# cluster-SELECTION ML study. Each shard writes
#   <sample>/<sample>_<seltag>training.shard<i>of<N>.root
# holding three TTrees: clusters, tracks, jets.
#
# Setup (once):
#   cd build && cmake .. && make export_training_data   # build BEFORE submitting
#   chmod +x condor/run_analysis.sh
#
# Submit -- one condor_submit per sample, from inside condor/ so the relative
# paths below resolve. Shard counts track EVENT count, not file count:
#
#   cd condor
#   condor_submit -a sample=vbf          -a nshards=10 export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=zjets        -a nshards=13 export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=dijet        -a nshards=5  export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=ttbar        -a nshards=20 export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=vbf_mu0      -a nshards=12 export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=zeejets_mu0  -a nshards=4  export_training_data_sharded.sub
#   condor_submit -a sample=ttbar_mu0    -a nshards=12 export_training_data_sharded.sub
#
# SHARD COUNTS TRACK EVENTS *READ*, NOT EVENTS ACCEPTED. The event loop runs over
# every event in the sample and only then applies the selection, so a sample with
# a 1.5% acceptance costs the same per shard as one with 20%. An earlier version
# of this list sized Z+jets from its accepted count and asked for 4 shards where
# its 2.0M read events want 13.
#
# The mu=0 samples are the exception to the events/150k rule: with no pileup they
# carry ~30x less data per event (0.0095 MB/ev against 0.285), so a shard gets
# through far more of them per unit time. 12 for the ~4.8M-event ones is ample.
#
# Those counts follow CLAUDE.md's "shards ~ events / 150,000" rule against the
# event totals measured on 2026-08-26 (vbf 1.46M, dijet 0.70M, zjets 2.0M) and
# the mu=0 totals in results/baseline_deta0p0_mjj500p0.md (vbf_mu0 4.75M,
# ttbar_mu0 4.93M, zeejets_mu0 1.05M). There is a ~50 s cvmfs floor per job, so
# splitting below ~5 min of work per shard buys little.
#
# ttbar (mu=200) has NEVER been run here, so its 20 is an ESTIMATE, not a
# measurement. On-disk size per event is strikingly uniform across the three
# measured mu=200 samples -- dijet 200 GB / 699,500 ev, vbf 418 GB / 1,464,400,
# zjets 571 GB / 2,000,000, i.e. 0.2854-0.2859 MB/ev -- and ttbar is 797 GB over
# 1000 files, which puts it near 2.8M events and so at ~19 shards.
#
# Treat that as a starting point: ttbar at mu=200 has a busier final state than
# any of the three, so its bytes/event could well run higher and its event count
# lower. Confirm from its own first run, where the "[chain] N files added" line
# and the run's event total give it for free.
#
# MERGE WITH hadd, NOT util/hist_merge:
#   cd ..
#   hadd -f condor/vbf/vbf_training.root condor/vbf/vbf_training.shard*of10.root
#
# That is the opposite of the rule for clustering_hist/rpt_v5_hist, and the
# reason is the file contents. hist_merge exists because those files carry
# TParameter scalars (event counts, pT accumulators) which have no Merge()
# method, so hadd would keep the FIRST input's copy and silently pair
# N-shard-summed histograms with one shard's event count. This file holds only
# TTrees, which hadd merges correctly by concatenating rows -- and hist_merge
# would refuse them, since it recognises histograms and scalars only.
#
# Glob on the shard COUNT (shard*of10), not on shard*: a directory that has
# been exported at two different shard counts holds both sets, and shard*
# matches both and double-counts every event.
#
# Validated on the local 33-file VBF sample: a 4-way sharded run hadd'd back
# together is bit-identical to the unsharded run in all three trees (96/96,
# 31/31, 13/13 columns), with event/row counts summing exactly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
universe                 = vanilla
executable               = run_analysis.sh

sample                   = vbf
nshards                  = 4

# Non-default VBS selection (resolveSelection in src/sample_config.h). These
# two MUST agree: selargs changes the cuts, and the executable stamps a
# matching SELECTION_TAG onto the output FILENAME so a loosened run cannot
# overwrite the standard one. transfer_output_files has to name that same file.
# seltag is that tag INCLUDING its trailing underscore.
#
#   selargs                          seltag
#   (empty)  [= no |Deta|, mjj>=200]  (empty)
#   --vbs-mjj=500                     mjj500p0_
#   --vbs-deta=3 --vbs-mjj=0          deta3p0_mjj0p0_
#   --vbs-deta=-1  [the LOOSE one]    novbs_
#
# A NEGATIVE --vbs-deta is a third mode, not a smaller threshold: it drops the
# VBS candidate-PAIR requirement outright, so an event with no
# opposite-hemisphere jet pair is kept. No combination of the two thresholds
# expresses that -- the pair's |Deta| and m_jj keep a -1 sentinel when no pair
# exists, so both fail any >= 0 test. It is tagged "novbs" alone, with no m_jj
# component, because naming a cut that is not being applied would describe a
# selection that does not exist.
#
# This is the only way to widen the Z+jets training population meaningfully
# (--vbs-deta=0 was measured worth +4%), which makes it the export to run for
# the "is Z+jets data-limited?" question -- see results/README.md.
#
# THE CANONICAL SELECTION FOR THIS STUDY IS --vbs-mjj=500, so the defaults
# below are NOT empty. results/baseline_deta0p0_mjj500p0.md is the baseline
# every model here is measured against, and it was taken at that selection; an
# export at the default mjj>=200 would train across a different fiducial region
# than the baseline it is compared to. Override both to change it.
selargs                  = --vbs-mjj=500
seltag                   = mjj500p0_

arguments                = export_training_data $(sample) --file-shard=$(ProcId)/$(nshards) $(selargs)

should_transfer_files    = YES
when_to_transfer_output  = ON_EXIT
transfer_input_files     = ../build/export_training_data
transfer_output_files    = $(sample)/$(sample)_$(seltag)training.shard$(ProcId)of$(nshards).root
# Same remap rationale as clustering_hist.sub: condor flattens a nested
# transfer_output_files path to its basename when staging back, so the remap is
# what makes the file land under $(sample)/ on the submit side. The LHS must be
# a BARE FILENAME -- per condor_submit(1), "You cannot specify directories to
# be remapped" -- while directories are allowed on the RHS and are created as
# needed.
#
# Note the executable calls histFilePath("training.root") with the UNPREFIXED
# base name; histFilePath() adds the "<sample>_" prefix, the selection tag and
# the shard tag itself. Passing an already-prefixed name would produce
# vbf/vbf_vbf_training.root and this remap would silently match nothing.
transfer_output_remaps   = "$(sample)_$(seltag)training.shard$(ProcId)of$(nshards).root=$(sample)/$(sample)_$(seltag)training.shard$(ProcId)of$(nshards).root"

getenv                   = True

# Write .out/.err back as they are produced rather than only on exit, so
# condor_tail on a running job shows real progress. Same rationale as
# clustering_hist.sub.
stream_output            = True
stream_error             = True

+JobFlavour              = "tomorrow"

# Sequential TTreeReader loop -- this executable has no TTreeProcessorMT, so
# there is nothing to parallelize within a job and request_cpus stays at 1.
# Parallelism here comes from sharding across jobs instead. (That is also why
# no <threads> positional argument is passed above.)
#
# Memory/disk are above the clustering_hist values because this writes TTrees
# rather than histograms: the per-track tree dominates at ~29 rows per passing
# event, and ROOT buffers baskets for ~140 branches across three trees before
# flushing. Sharding divides the per-job output size by $(nshards), so these
# are generous for a sharded run and sized for the nshards=1 fallback.
request_cpus             = 1
request_memory           = 6GB
request_disk             = 24GB

output = logs/export_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).out
error  = logs/export_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).err
log    = logs/export_$(sample).$(ClusterId).$(ProcId).log

# $(ProcId) is the shard index: this runs ProcId 0..nshards-1, which
# --file-shard consumes directly.
queue $(nshards)
